! The PAW silicon of si-epsilon-paw.in on an unshifted 2x2x2 grid, reduced to its wedge.
!
! Half of a pair; the other half is si-epsilon-paw-unshifted-nosym.in, the same
! k-sample whole with no symmetry. An unshifted grid is closed under the point
! group, so the two routes are the same calculation and must give the same
! dynamical matrix to round-off. For a norm-conserving dataset that check is
! si-epsilon-unshifted.in; a PAW dataset adds what that pair cannot reach, the
! one-centre energy as a second functional of `becsum`, whose response on a wedge
! is symmetrised apart from the density's.
!
! `ecutrho` is 180 rather than si-epsilon-paw.in's 160 for one reason: at 160 the
! wedge's dense grid is 32^3 (diamond's quarter translations make it a multiple of
! four) and the whole grid's is 30^3, which is a difference of basis rather than of
! symmetrisation. At 180 both are 32^3, and both smooth grids 24^3.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 10.20, nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 20.0, ecutrho = 180.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-12
    mixing_beta = 0.7
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-n-kjpaw_psl.0.1.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 2 2 2 0 0 0
